            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: Texts and data</b><br /><br />Some old stories say that the universe was made for people. Scientific measurements sometimes tell a different story.<br /><br />Does that mean the ancient epics are wrong?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Literature and science\n\nCan a poem or an epic really teach us something about science?\n\nSome people say that literature (epics and poems) and science have nothing to do with each other. That is not quite right. Literary texts are often labeled too quickly, and that makes the confusion worse.\n\nTo help you see this more clearly, History Tracers is building the section *Understanding Texts* (<htcite0>). There we look closely at the relationship between science and literature (<htcite1>), including the border between poetry and fact (<htcite2>).\n\nWhen we quickly label something as “right” or “wrong,” we often miss how complex texts really are (<htcite3>; <htcite4>; <htcite5>). If we study scientific data and literary works carefully, we can see that both can be correct — each one in its own context.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2806b294-0d2a-4ca2-8008-b2b82791427a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding texts",
